    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a good start. Can you expand a bit on the potential risks or \"pain points\" for each method? Specifically, I want to highlight why vertical scaling might become a bottleneck or a single point of failure during a sudden traffic spike, whereas horizontal scaling might introduce more complexity in terms of data consistency or networking.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Vertical scaling risks a hard ceiling; if the traffic spike exceeds the largest available instance size, the service fails entirely, creating a single point of bottleneck failure. In contrast, horizontal scaling introduces operational complexity, as we must ensure that distributed instances remain consistent, especially when managing shared state or session data across many nodes. While horizontal scaling is more resilient to failure, it requires robust load balancing and data management strategies to prevent data inconsistency as services spread out. Therefore, our strategy should favor horizontal scaling while mitigating its distributed system complexities.",
      "source": "target",
